{"background_tasks":[],"cwd":"[SCRATCH]/projects/compact","hook_event_name":"Stop","last_assistant_message":"A config file is a text or structured data file that stores configuration settings for an application or system. Instead of hardcoding values directly into the source code, developers use config files to externalize settings like database connection strings, API endpoints, logging levels, feature flags, or server ports. This separation allows the same application to run in different environments (development, staging, production) with different configurations without requiring code changes or recompilation.\n\nConfig files come in various formats including JSON, YAML, TOML, INI, and XML, each with different syntaxes and use cases. They're typically read when an application starts up, making it easy to adjust behavior by simply editing the file—useful for operations teams who need to tune settings without involving developers. Common examples include `.env` files for environment variables, `settings.json` for application preferences, `docker-compose.yml` for container orchestration, and `nginx.conf` for web server configuration. By centralizing configuration in files, teams gain flexibility, maintainability, and the ability to manage different deployments efficiently.","permission_mode":"default","prompt_id":"15acd794-76b9-46b0-b2c3-cb4b8465192d","session_crons":[],"session_id":"e4977db8-f0a7-4f20-a1f7-46b852eb105e","stop_hook_active":false,"transcript_path":"[HOME]/.claude/projects/[SCRATCH-SLUG]-projects-compact/e4977db8-f0a7-4f20-a1f7-46b852eb105e.jsonl","type":"hook.stop"}
